From 7fa5ab61f04b0cfce2cbae54e12ebe2b733b768f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 04:19:17 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 11/28] descriptor: add the provisional tmr() P2MR descriptor

BIP 360 defines the P2MR output format but no descriptor language. Spell a
P2MR script tree as tmr(TREE), the name used by the cryptoquick/bitcoin
prototype, and mark it provisional in the documentation: it is not specified
by any BIP and may change.

TREE reuses the tr() tree syntax and its leaves are parsed in the P2TR
context, since BIP 360 executes them under the BIP 342 tapscript rules with
leaf version 0xc0. There is no internal key argument because there is no key
path: the witness program is the script tree Merkle root itself.

A tree with a single leaf is refused. Such a leaf sits at depth zero, so its
control block has m = 0, and BIP 360 then succeeds without executing the leaf
script, which would make the output anyone-can-spend. The error message points
at addr() and raw() for expressing that output deliberately.

BIP 360 reuses the BIP 341 TapLeaf/TapBranch tree verbatim, so TaprootBuilder
computes both output types; it gains GetP2MROutput() and GetP2MRSpendData(),
which differ from the taproot versions only in the control block prefix (one
leaf version byte instead of leaf version plus internal key) and in committing
to the root directly rather than to a tweaked key. For the same reason the
tree reconstruction in InferTaprootTree() is factored into a helper shared
with the new InferP2MRTree(), parameterised by the control block prefix size.

SigningProvider, HidingSigningProvider, FlatSigningProvider and
MultiSigningProvider learn GetP2MRSpendData()/GetP2MRBuilder(), and
InferScript() reconstructs a tmr() descriptor for a P2MR output whose tree is
known, falling back to addr() otherwise.
